WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.440
One blood sample gives you one number.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
What can it tell you?

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.560
A hormone level is one point on a moving curve.

4
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.680
Hormones are released in pulses, then cleared.

5
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.100
Growth hormone left plasma with an average half-life of twenty-seven minutes.

6
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.520
That was one small study's mean, after a large intravenous dose in five people.

7
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.620
A sample drawn just before a pulse can read many times lower than one drawn on its

8
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:28.620
peak.

9
00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:30.663
So read two hormones together.

10
00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:32.423
Take the cortisol axis.

11
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:37.743
CRH, helped by vasopressin, drives ACTH from the pituitary.

12
00:00:38.323 --> 00:00:41.443
ACTH drives the adrenal cortex to make cortisol.

13
00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:45.843
Call ACTH the trophic hormone and cortisol the target hormone.

14
00:00:46.703 --> 00:00:50.023
Cortisol then feeds back on CRH and ACTH.

15
00:00:50.023 --> 00:00:53.583
That is long-loop feedback, with strong evidence at both levels.

16
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.287
Now break the chain at the gland.

17
00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:01.307
In this made-up example, adults on a normal sleep-wake schedule have blood drawn at

18
00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:09.267
08:00. Person A has cortisol of 3 µg per dL and ACTH of 300 pg per mL.

19
00:01:10.027 --> 00:01:13.767
Low cortisol should make an intact pituitary drive hard, and it does.

20
00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:17.367
The adrenal does not answer: that is the primary pattern.

21
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.727
Reference intervals differ by laboratory and assay.

22
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:27.797
Person B has the same low cortisol, but ACTH is only 5 pg per mL.

23
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:29.897
The drive itself is missing.

24
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:35.557
That points to the pituitary, a secondary disorder, or to the hypothalamus, a

25
00:01:35.557 --> 00:01:41.097
tertiary one. The pair cannot say which, because either lesion lowers ACTH.

26
00:01:41.097 --> 00:01:43.917
It can separate gland disease from central disease.

27
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.730
Now change one input and watch the output.

28
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.380
Person B receives synthetic ACTH.

29
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.650
Thirty minutes later, cortisol is 16 µg per dL.

30
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.510
Whether that is adequate depends on the assay, because criteria for newer, more

31
00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.590
specific assays sit below the historical one.

32
00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.650
An adequate response shows only that the adrenal can answer ACTH.

33
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:12.850
After a recent loss of central drive, the adrenal cortex may not yet have atrophied.

34
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.850
So a normal result does not exclude a recent pituitary or hypothalamic defect.

35
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.993
The thyroid reads the same way.

36
00:02:22.613 --> 00:02:26.553
High TSH with low free T4 is the classical primary pattern.

37
00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:32.373
Low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH points to central disease.

38
00:02:33.073 --> 00:02:37.813
A hormone level is a point on a moving curve, and a criterion belongs to its assay.

39
00:02:38.573 --> 00:02:41.973
Read hormones as pairs, and know which assay made the number.
